<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Keeping Our Communities Clean – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Bring the Cleaning Supplies?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Brainstorm questions in a group.
      <br>
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a routine cleanup schedule (e.g., 'First, I study. Then, I clean my desk.').
      <br>Can brainstorm questions for organizing a cleanup (e.g., 'What supplies do we need?').
      <br>Can exchange information about recycling (e.g., 'When is recycling picked up?' 'It's on Monday.').
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows two pictures: one of a dirty park and another of a clean park. The teacher asks, "Which park do you like more? Why?" Students share their answers orally (e.g., "I like the clean park because it's beautiful."). The teacher then asks, "What can we do to make a dirty park clean?" Elicit responses like "We can pick up trash" or "We should clean." This activates prior knowledge related to community cleanup and encourages initial speaking.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up realia (e.g., a small toy broom, a small bag) or pictures of cleaning supplies. The teacher models simple questions and answers for planning a cleanup: "What supplies do we need?" (pointing to a broom) "We need a broom." "Who will bring the bags?" "Maria will bring the bags." The teacher clearly pronounces imperative commands like 'Bring the trash bags!' and 'Clean the area!' with appropriate word stress. The teacher also models short and long vowel sounds in words like 'bring' (/ɪ/) and 'clean' (/iː/).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using CCQs:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Bring a bag!', what should you do? (Show me with your hands)." (Expected: Students mime bringing a bag.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we say 'We should clean the park', does it mean 'we must clean' or 'it's a good idea to clean'?" (Expected: 'It's a good idea to clean.')</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students, Ana and Leo, planning a schoolyard cleanup. The dialogue incorporates modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense (will), imperatives, and cleanup vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Ana: "Hi Leo! We should organize a cleanup for our schoolyard next week."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "That’s a great idea, Ana! What supplies do we need?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Ana: "We will need trash bags and gloves. You can bring the trash bags, and I will bring the gloves."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Okay. And what about recycling? When is recycling picked up?"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Ana: "It's on Monday. We must sort the recycling carefully."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Good. We should also ask other volunteers to help. Gather the team!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information; making suggestions; describing plans and tasks. (Pragmatic and Sociolinguistic: Participate in group discussions on community actions).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue (teacher reads it aloud or plays an audio) and answer simple oral questions:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Ana and Leo's plan?" (Expected: To organize a cleanup.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What supplies will they need?" (Expected: Trash bags and gloves.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is recycling picked up?" (Expected: On Monday.)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense (will), imperatives, question words (what, how, where), and cleanup vocabulary to brainstorm questions and exchange information orally.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides incomplete sentences on the board or flashcards, and students complete them orally with the correct modal verb or imperative, focusing on pronunciation of target words.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We ____ clean the park next Saturday." (should / will)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You ____ help by collecting the recycling." (can / must)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"____ a bag for trash!" (Bring / Clean)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Volunteers ____ wear gloves." (must / can)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Question Formation Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence prompts or keywords. Students transform them into questions using appropriate question words and modal verbs.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "supplies / need?" -> Student 1: "What supplies do we need?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "who / bring / tools?" -> Student 2: "Who will bring the tools?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt: "where / recycle / bottles?" -> Student 1: "Where can we recycle bottles?"</li>
            </ul>
            Teacher circulates, listens for correct usage and provides immediate feedback on grammar and pronunciation (word stress on imperatives like 'bring', short/long vowel sounds in 'clean' /iː/ vs 'bin' /ɪ/).
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Information Exchange:</b> Students, in pairs, ask and answer questions about hypothetical cleanup tasks. They use the learned vocabulary and grammar. One student has a list of tasks (e.g., 'sweep the streets', 'organize the recycling', 'collect trash bags'), and the other student asks 'who' and 'what' questions.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Who will sweep the streets?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Maria will sweep the streets. What should I bring?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "You should bring a broom. When will we collect trash bags?"</li>
            </ul>
            This activity encourages fluid conversational practice.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group brainstorming session for a community cleanup project (groups of 3-4 students).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups and gives each group a scenario card, e.g., "Your group needs to plan a cleanup for the park next week." or "Your group needs to organize a recycling project for the neighborhood."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to brainstorm questions that they need to answer to organize the cleanup project. They must use question words (what, how, where), modal verbs (can, should, must), and future tense (will).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and writes down a list of at least 5-7 questions to plan their project. For example:
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What supplies do we need?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who will bring the trash bags?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where should we put the recycling?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When will we clean?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can we get more volunteers?"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We must organize teams. What teams should we have?"</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on group discussion and collaboration, allowing students to freely use the target language to achieve the specific objective: "Brainstorm questions in a group."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors groups, encouraging all members to speak and providing support without over-correcting, prioritizing fluency and communicative success.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for group participation and use of target language. Peer assessment during group presentations.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their brainstormed list of questions to the class. One or two students from each group can present, or they can take turns. Other groups can ask follow-up questions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Word stress on imperatives (e.g., 'Bring!', 'Clean!') and clear distinction between short and long vowel sounds (e.g., 'bin' /ɪ/ vs. 'clean' /iː/).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., supplies, trash, recycling, volunteers, organize, clean).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense (will), and imperatives in questions and statements.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of questions:</b> Are the questions understandable and relevant to planning a cleanup?</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute to the discussion and presentation? (Teacher observation)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One question I asked in my group was: __________."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel (happy/okay/not good) about asking questions in English because _________."</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new questions can you ask to organize a cleanup now?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about talking in your group today?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding students brainstorming questions and using modal verbs and future tense? (e.g., "The group work fostered good interaction and students enjoyed planning.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time to improve speaking fluency and accuracy? (e.g., "Some students still struggled with question formation; more sentence frame practice might be needed.")</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job, everyone! We planned a fantastic cleanup today by asking many important questions. Next time (Lesson 4), we will take these ideas and learn how to write a short plan for our community cleanup project."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>